/**
  @page examples-pdes-elasticity Solving the elasticity equation
  @brief Compute and visualize the displacement of a clamped beam under traction.

  A cantilever is held at one end and pulled downward at the other. This example
  constructs its mesh, assigns boundary labels, solves a vector-valued linear
  elasticity problem, and exports the displacement for visualization.

  The @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson example" introduces the same workflow
  for a scalar field. Here, the unknown has two components, and different parts
  of the boundary carry different conditions.

  @m_keywords{Elasticity}
  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-pdes-elasticity-intro The mathematical problem

  The beam occupies the rectangle @f$\Omega=(0,4)\times(0,1)@f$. Its displacement
  @f$u:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}^2@f$ describes how each point moves from its original
  position. The boundary is divided into three parts:

  - **Fixed end:** @f$\Gamma_D=\{0\}\times[0,1]@f$, where @f$u=0@f$.
  - **Loaded end:** @f$\Gamma_N=\{4\}\times[0,1]@f$, with the uniform traction
    @f$g=(0,-10^{-4})@f$.
  - **Free sides:** the top and bottom edges, denoted by @f$\Gamma_0@f$, with
    zero traction.

  There is no body force. In the small-strain, isotropic model, the strain and
  stress are
  @f[
    \varepsilon(u)=\tfrac12\bigl(\nabla u+\nabla u^T\bigr),
    \qquad
    \sigma(u)=\lambda\,\operatorname{div}(u)I+2\mu\varepsilon(u).
  @f]
  The Lamé parameters are @f$\lambda=0.5769@f$ and @f$\mu=0.3846@f$.
  These are used directly in a two-dimensional plane-strain model; the geometry
  and material data are illustrative nondimensional values. The small applied
  load keeps the displacement small relative to the beam dimensions.

  With @f$n@f$ the outward unit normal, equilibrium requires
  @f[
    \begin{aligned}
      -\operatorname{div}\sigma(u)&=0 &&\text{in }\Omega,\\
      u&=0 &&\text{on }\Gamma_D,\\
      \sigma(u)n&=g &&\text{on }\Gamma_N,\\
      \sigma(u)n&=0 &&\text{on }\Gamma_0.
    \end{aligned}
  @f]
  This formulation describes linear elastic response; it does not model large
  rotations or nonlinear material behavior. See @cite allaire2021shape for
  further background on elasticity and its variational formulation.

  @section examples-pdes-elasticity-weak The weak formulation

  Let @f$V=\{v\in H^1(\Omega)^2:v=0\text{ on }\Gamma_D\}@f$. Multiplying
  equilibrium by a test displacement and integrating by parts gives: find
  @f$u\in V@f$ such that, for every @f$v\in V@f$,
  @f[
    \int_\Omega \lambda\,\operatorname{div}(u)\operatorname{div}(v)
      +2\mu\,\varepsilon(u):\varepsilon(v)\,dx
    =\int_{\Gamma_N}g\cdot v\,ds.
  @f]
  The colon denotes the componentwise inner product of two tensors. The left
  side is the elastic stiffness; the right side is the work of the applied
  traction. The free sides contribute no load. Fixing the entire left edge
  removes rigid-body motions and makes the discrete stiffness positive definite
  for these material parameters.

  @section examples-pdes-elasticity-impl-setup 1. Construct and label the mesh

  `UniformGrid` takes vertex counts and initially uses unit spacing. A grid
  with 41 vertices horizontally and 11 vertically, scaled by @f$0.1@f$, therefore
  covers the required rectangle with 451 vertices and 800 triangles.

  @snippet PDEs/Elasticity.cpp elasticity-mesh

  Edge-to-cell connectivity identifies exterior edges. The loop labels an edge
  as `fixed` when both endpoints lie on the left end, `loaded` when both lie on
  the right end, and `free` otherwise. The coordinate tolerance is only used
  to recognize these boundaries on this generated grid. The numerical label
  values have no physical meaning; the form below gives them their roles.

  @section examples-pdes-elasticity-impl-problem 2. Express the finite element problem

  The vector-valued `P1` space uses continuous, piecewise affine displacements
  with two components per vertex: 902 degrees of freedom before imposing the
  boundary values. `u` is the trial displacement and `v` is the test function.

  @snippet PDEs/Elasticity.cpp elasticity-form

  `LinearElasticityIntegral` includes both stiffness terms in the weak form,
  including the factor @f$2\mu@f$ multiplying the strain inner product.
  `BoundaryIntegral(...).over(loaded)` restricts the traction to the right end;
  `DirichletBC(...).on(fixed)` fixes both displacement components on the left.
  No boundary integral is needed on the free sides: zero traction is the
  natural condition there.

  Rodin expresses the equation as a residual, so the external work is subtracted
  from the stiffness term. The boundary condition completes the problem.

  @section examples-pdes-elasticity-impl-solve 3. Solve and check convergence

  Conjugate gradients is suitable for the constrained symmetric positive
  definite system. The solver assembles the problem, solves to the requested
  relative tolerance, and checks success before exporting any result.

  @snippet PDEs/Elasticity.cpp elasticity-solve

  The computed displacement field is available through `u.getSolution()`.
  Solver convergence checks the algebraic solve; a mesh refinement study would
  be needed to assess discretization error for a chosen quantity of interest.

  @section examples-pdes-elasticity-output 4. Export and visualize the displacement

  XDMF stores the mesh and vector field together. Keep the accompanying HDF5
  files alongside `Elasticity.xdmf`, as they contain the numerical data.

  @snippet PDEs/Elasticity.cpp elasticity-export

  @m_class{m-flat}
  @image html PDEs/Elasticity.png "Computed beam displacement, amplified ten times. Color shows the unscaled displacement magnitude; the dashed outline marks the original beam." width=720px

  In ParaView:

  1. Open `Elasticity.xdmf` and select **Apply**.
  2. Apply **Warp By Vector**, using **displacement** and a scale factor of **10**.
  3. Color by the **Magnitude** of **displacement**.
  4. Select **Surface With Edges** to inspect the triangular mesh.

  The clamped end remains stationary and the free end bends downward. For this
  mesh and load, the maximum nodal displacement magnitude is approximately
  @f$0.023787@f$, and the vertical displacement at the loaded end is approximately
  @f$-0.0234@f$. The amplification in the figure changes only the displayed
  geometry, not the computed field or its color scale.

  @section examples-pdes-elasticity-full Complete program

  Build the `Elasticity` target with the Rodin examples enabled, then run it from
  the directory in which the output should be written. The program generates
  its own mesh and requires no external mesh file.

  @snippet PDEs/Elasticity.cpp elasticity-source

  See @ref PDEs/Elasticity.cpp "Elasticity.cpp" for the source,
  the @ref guides-getting-started-installation "installation guide" for build
  setup, and the @ref guides-io "I/O guide" for further export options.

  @example PDEs/Elasticity.cpp @m_examplenavigation{examples-pdes-elasticity,PDEs/} @m_footernavigation
*/
